## Why This Is Asked
This probes understanding of iterative approaches, partition logic, and complexity trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort. 

## Key Concepts
- Iterative quicksort with explicit stack
- Hoare partition scheme and pivot strategy
- Time/space complexity comparisons with mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
function iterativeQuicksort(a){
  const n=a.length;
  const stack=[[0,n-1]];
  const swap=(i,j)=>{let t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  while(stack.length){
    const [l,r]=stack.pop();
    if(l>=r) continue;
    let pivot=a[(l+r)>>1];
    let i=l, j=r;
    while(i<=j){
      while(a[i]<pivot) i++;
      while(a[j]>pivot) j--;
      if(i<=j){ swap(i,j); i++; j--; }
    }
    if(l<j) stack.push([l,j]);
    if(i<r) stack.push([i,r]);
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to guarantee O(n log n) worst-case?
- Compare stability with mergesort; when would you choose one over the other?

## Why This Is Asked
This question blends BFS reachability, Dijkstra shortest paths, and counting shortest paths via a DAG and topological order.

## Key Concepts
- BFS/DFS reachability
- Dijkstra's algorithm
- Shortest-path DAG
- Topological DP and modular counting

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, adj, s) {
  const dist = Array(n).fill(Infinity); dist[s] = 0;
  // priority queue based Dijkstra (pseudo-notation)
  const pq = new MinPQ((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]);
  pq.enqueue([0,s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d,u] = pq.dequeue(); if (d!==dist[u]) continue;
    for (const {to:v, w} of adj[u]){
      if (dist[v] > d + w){ dist[v] = d + w; pq.enqueue([dist[v], v]); }
    }
  }
  const dag = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>[]);
  for (let u=0; u<n; u++) for (const {to:v, w} of adj[u]) if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) dag[u].push(v);
  const order = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i).sort((a,b)=>dist[a]-dist[b]);
  const MOD = 1000000007; const ways = Array(n).fill(0); ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) for (const v of dag[u]) ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD;
  return {dist, ways};
}
```

